# {{.Name}}

{{- if .API}}

An API-only [Forge](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go) application
(`forge new --api`): JSON handlers, with no views, assets, JavaScript,
translations, sign-in pages or browser middleware.
{{- else}}

A [Forge](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go) application.
{{- end}}

{{- if and .DB (not .API)}}

## Quickstart

From an empty directory to a working CRUD at `/products`, with no code to
edit: the generators mount their routes in `app/bootstrap/routes_gen.go`.

```bash
{{.NewCommand}}
cd {{.Name}}
{{- if .DB.Server}}
docker compose up -d --wait {{.DB.Service}}
{{- end}}
forge migrate
{{- if and .DB.Identity .Tenants}}
forge setup --org acme --email you@example.com
forge generate scaffold product name:string price:integer --org-scoped
{{- else if .DB.Identity}}
forge setup --email you@example.com
forge generate scaffold product name:string price:integer
{{- else}}
forge generate scaffold product name:string price:integer --public
{{- end}}
forge migrate
forge dev
```

{{- if .DB.Identity}}

`forge setup` asks for the owner's password twice (or reads
`FORGE_SETUP_PASSWORD`). Then open `http://localhost:8080/session/new`, sign
in{{if .Tenants}} with organization `acme`{{end}}, and manage products at
`http://localhost:8080/products`: the pages and the JSON API at
`/v1/products` need sign-in and the `products:read` and `products:write`
permissions, which the scaffold's migration grants to the owner role. The JSON
API authenticates the same session cookie{{if .APIAuth}} or a bearer token from `POST /auth/login`{{end}};
changes send the CSRF token in the `X-CSRF-Token` header:

```bash
jar=$(mktemp)
token=$(curl -s -c "$jar" -H 'Accept: text/html' http://localhost:8080/session/new | sed -n 's/.*name="authenticity_token" value="\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -n 1)
read -rsp 'Password: ' password; echo
curl -s -o /dev/null -b "$jar" -c "$jar" -H 'Accept: text/html' --data-urlencode "authenticity_token=$token"{{if .Tenants}} --data-urlencode tenant=acme{{end}} --data-urlencode email=you@example.com --data-urlencode "password=$password" http://localhost:8080/session
curl -s -b "$jar" http://localhost:8080/v1/products
curl -s -b "$jar" -H "X-CSRF-Token: $(awk '$6 == "__Host-forge_csrf" {print $7}' "$jar")" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"Lamp","price":1999}' http://localhost:8080/v1/products
```
{{- else}}

Then open `http://localhost:8080/products`, and call the JSON API at
`/v1/products`:

```bash
curl -s http://localhost:8080/v1/products
curl -s -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"Lamp","price":1999}' http://localhost:8080/v1/products
```

This application has no authentication (it was created without `--auth`;
`forge generate authentication` adds the sign-in pages and `forge setup`), so
its generated routes are closed unless generated with
`--public`, which serves them to anyone who can reach the server, without
sign-in (forms still need their CSRF token). Use it for prototypes on your
machine. To put your own authentication in front, copy the `public`
function of `app/bootstrap/routes_gen.go` into another file of
`app/bootstrap`, change it there, and run any `forge generate` command again
(repeating the last one is enough): until then the build fails with `public
redeclared`, afterwards the generators use yours. Without `--public` the
routes answer `403` until you replace `protect` the same way.
{{- end}}

Generated routes live in `app/bootstrap/routes_gen.go`, which `forge generate
scaffold`, `resource` and `controller` rewrite and `forge destroy` takes their
blocks back out of; do not edit it (they refuse to touch it once its
checksum no longer matches). Running a generator again with the same
arguments changes nothing.
{{- end}}

