{{- if .DB}}
{{- if .DB.Server}}

This starts {{.DB.Title}}, applies migrations with a one-off `migrate`
container, then starts the app. The database is published on
`127.0.0.1:{{.DBHostPort}}`, a port derived from the application name so it does not
clash with a locally installed server; change it in both `compose.yaml` and
`.env.development` if needed.
{{- if .DevMigrationURL}}

On its first start, `docker/postgres/init.sql` creates the database and two
development-only roles: `app_migrator`, which owns the schema and applies
migrations (`FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL`, given only to the `migrate`
container), and `app_runtime`, which the app serves with
(`FORGE_DATABASE_URL`): no superuser, no `BYPASSRLS`, no ownership, only data
privileges, so row-level security applies to it and it cannot change the
schema. `.env.development` uses the same roles; tests connect as the
`postgres` superuser to create their own databases and roles, and run the
repositories as a fresh runtime role. To start over (this re-runs
`init.sql`):

```bash
docker compose down --volumes
```
{{- end}}
{{- else}}

This applies migrations with a one-off `migrate` container, then starts the
app. The SQLite database lives on the `storage` volume; locally, `forge dev`
uses `storage/development.db`.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

