and are added as the application grows.

## Architecture

Code is layered in rings and imports point inward only:

| Ring | May import |
| --- | --- |
| `app/domain` | itself, the standard library without `net`, `net/http`, `database/sql`, `os`, `io/fs`, `io/ioutil`, `path/filepath`, `embed`, `html/template`, `unsafe` and cgo, and the framework's `uuid` and `fault` |
| `app/application` | `app/domain`, itself, the same standard library subset, the framework's `uuid`, `fault`, `pagination` and `tenancy` |
| `app/adapters` | the inner rings and the framework; never `app/bootstrap` |
| `app/bootstrap` | everything: the only place that wires the application |

`app/jobs`, `app/mailers`{{if not .API}}, `app/views`{{end}}, `db` and `config` sit outside the rings
and, like the adapters, never import `app/bootstrap`. `app/architecture_test.go`
parses the imports of every Go file, test files included, with the standard
library and fails with the file, the line and the forbidden import. It runs with `forge test` and in CI.

```bash
forge test ./app/ -run TestArchitecture -v    # only the architecture test
forge test                                    # everything, including it
```

To add a third-party package to the domain or application rings, list it in
`extraAllowedImports` in that file, in a reviewed commit.

`AGENTS.md` is the same rules, the commands, the routes seam and the security
defaults written for AI coding agents; keep it in step when you change the
layout. A tool that reads only `CLAUDE.md` can import it with a one-line
`CLAUDE.md` that contains `@AGENTS.md`.
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity}}

## Identity

The application composes Forge's identity migrations (`auth{{.DB.Adapter}}` in
`app/bootstrap/app_gen.go`) with its own, and `app/bootstrap/identity.go`
wires the {{.DB.Title}} identity store, the sign-in pages and the setup
command into the composition root.

{{- if .APIAuth}}

The generated API (`--api-auth`) exposes:

- `POST /auth/login` for tenant-scoped email/password bearer login;
- `POST /auth/logout` for idempotent bearer-session revocation with security
  audit;
- `GET /v1/auth/session` as an authenticated example returning the current
  subject, tenant, and permissions.
{{- end}}

The generated `.env.local` contains a unique development-only key for local
MFA seed encryption. It is git-ignored and loaded by both `forge` and Docker
Compose. Never reuse this development key in production; configure a separate
secret there. If `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` is configured, the generated app wires
the `/session/mfa` step{{if .APIAuth}} and `POST /auth/mfa/complete`{{end}}. The value must be canonical unpadded base64url for
exactly 32 random bytes and belongs in `.env.local` during development or a
secret manager in deployed environments. It is never stored in the identity
database.

Run `migrate` before serving so the Forge identity tables exist, then
`setup` once to create the first owner, sign in and open `/mfa` to turn on
two-factor authentication. Inviting further users remains an application
opt-in. The session cookies need HTTPS (or `localhost`). Password recovery delivery is
enabled by the `FORGE_MAIL_*` and `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL` settings
above; any SMTP server works, self-hosted included.
{{- end}}

## API description (OpenAPI)

JSON routes declare their request, response and error types next to the
route (`openapi.Typed` or `openapi.Describe` from
`github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/openapi`), so the OpenAPI 3.1 document
is generated from the Go types and cannot drift from them. `GET /v1/status`
in `app/bootstrap` is the example; generated resources and `--api`
controllers are described too{{if and .DB .DB.Identity .APIAuth}}, and so are the identity routes
(`/auth/login`, logout, MFA completion when `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` is set, password
recovery and the cookie routes): Forge's `auth` handlers carry their descriptions, so register them as
returned{{end}}.

```bash
# Served in development and test (FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true elsewhere)
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/openapi.json
{{- if and .DB .DB.Identity .APIAuth}}
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/openapi.json | grep '"/auth/'   # the identity routes
{{- end}}

# Written from the application without serving it{{if and .DB .DB.Server}} (needs the database, like forge migrate){{end}}
forge openapi --output openapi.json
forge openapi --check openapi.json   # exits 1 when the committed file is stale (CI)
```

In staging and production the document is not served unless
`FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true`: it lists every documented route. The handler
tests of generated resources check every response against it.
{{- if .DB}}

## Background jobs
{{if .DB.Jobs}}
`app/bootstrap/bootstrap.go` has `JobsConfigure`, which starts an in-process
`jobs.Scheduler` for recurring work and stops it within the shutdown timeout
(`FORGE_HTTP_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT`). Add a job with `forge generate job cleanup`,
then register it there with an explicit schedule. Every replica runs the
scheduler, and each tick of a job runs on exactly one replica: the first to
record it in the `forge_job_schedules` table (a framework migration applied by
`migrate`), so scaling out does not multiply runs. Recording a tick holds no
connection while the job runs and does not depend on the replicas' clocks.
Two replicas can run consecutive ticks of one job at the same time.
{{end}}
Work that must survive restarts and retry runs on the persistent job queue
(`queue/{{.DB.Adapter}}`), like Solid Queue in Rails:

```bash
forge generate job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue --dry-run   # preview the typed handler and its tests
forge generate job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue             # create app/jobs/send_welcome.go and its test
```

Follow the printed wiring, and add the queue's tables and the `jobs` command
to `cmd/{{.Name}}/main.go` (`queue{{.DB.Adapter}}.Migrations()` and
`queue{{.DB.Adapter}}.Commands()`; see the
[jobs guide](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/blob/main/docs/JOBS.md)).
Delivery is at least once: make handlers idempotent. A job out of attempts is
`dead` until you act. The application binary's `jobs` command inspects and
repairs the queue (payloads are never printed):

```bash
docker compose run --rm app jobs status                   # jobs per queue and state
docker compose run --rm app jobs dead                     # dead jobs; also -queue Q, -kind K, -limit N
docker compose run --rm app jobs show JOB_ID              # one job's metadata
docker compose run --rm app jobs retry JOB_ID             # run a dead job again with a fresh attempt budget
docker compose run --rm app jobs discard JOB_ID           # delete a dead job
docker compose run --rm app jobs prune -older 24h         # delete finished jobs
```

On a host deployed with `forge deploy`, run them as
`forge app exec jobs dead`.
{{- end}}

## Observability

`forge dev` writes a Rails-style log: a line per request (method, route,
status, time{{if .DB}}, number of SQL queries{{end}}){{if .DB}} and per SQL statement (the text, never the
arguments; yellow when slow){{end}}, in color on a terminal. A job line always
shows failures and retries; successes only for a worker built with
`queue.WorkerOptions{LogSuccesses: true}`. Production writes JSON with the same
event names (`duration` in nanoseconds), and the values of keys such as `password`,
`token`, `session` and `email` are `[REDACTED]` in every format. Redaction is by
key only: log identifiers, not objects, and never put a secret in a message:

```bash
forge dev                             # pretty on a terminal
NO_COLOR=1 forge dev                  # the same layout without colors
FORGE_LOG_FORMAT=pretty forge dev     # force it on a pipe or a file (text, json, auto)
{{- if .DB}}
FORGE_LOG_SLOW_QUERY=50ms forge dev   # statements slower than 50ms turn yellow (default 100ms)
{{- end}}
```

Every request gets a trace ID that appears as `trace_id` in its logs, and
HTTP{{if .DB}}, database{{end}} and job metrics are recorded. Nothing leaves the process
until you choose an export path:

```bash
# Prometheus scrape endpoint on a private port (GET /metrics)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9464 forge dev
# Traces and metrics to an OpenTelemetry Collector (OTLP/HTTP)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318 forge dev
# Spans in the development log
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=log forge dev
# Every log record, with trace_id and span_id, also to the collector (OTLP/HTTP);
# standard output keeps the same lines. Uses the same FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_* settings.
FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318 forge dev
```

Go runtime (goroutines, heap, garbage collection){{if .DB}} and database connection pool
(connections in use, idle and the limit, waits){{end}} metrics are always
recorded and reach both `/metrics` and OTLP.

Set `FORGE_TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME={{.Name}}` in deployments. Incoming
`traceparent` headers are ignored unless `FORGE_TELEMETRY_TRUST_TRACEPARENT=true`
(only behind a proxy that sets or sanitizes them). A metrics address other
than loopback, such as `0.0.0.0:9464` in a container, needs
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_PUBLIC=true`; never publish that port. See the Forge
observability guide (docs/OBSERVABILITY.md) for every variable.

### Observability stack: Grafana, Prometheus, Tempo and Loki

`compose.observability.yaml` adds a preconfigured stack to the compose
project: an OpenTelemetry Collector, Prometheus (metrics), Tempo (traces),
Loki (logs) and Grafana with the data sources and a starter dashboard already
provisioned. It is opt-in: `docker compose up` does not start it. Everything
stays on your machine and every published port is bound to `127.0.0.1`.

```bash
# 1. Start it (Grafana refuses to start without an admin password you choose)
export GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD="$(openssl rand -base64 18)"   # or put it in .env
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml up --build

# 2. Generate some data
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/status

# 3. Open Grafana: http://127.0.0.1:3000, user admin, the password above
#    Dashboards > "Forge application"  request rate, latency, errors, database, connection pool, Go runtime, jobs, logs, traces
#    Explore > Tempo                   traces; "Logs for this span" opens the matching log lines
#    Explore > Loki                    logs; a trace_id links to its trace
#    Explore > Prometheus              metrics, also at http://127.0.0.1:9090
#    http://127.0.0.1:9090/alerts      the alert rules of observability/alerts.yml and whether each fires

# 4. Stop it (keeps the data); add -v to delete traces, logs and metrics too
# compose interpolates the whole file, so GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD must be set for down too
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml down
```

`observability/alerts.yml` carries alert rules for the metrics the framework
exports: a target down or no metrics arriving, more than 5% of requests failing
with 5xx, p95 latency over 500 ms, database pool over 90% in use or callers
waiting for a connection, queue jobs that died and scheduled jobs that failed.
Edit the thresholds there and run `docker compose -f compose.yaml -f
compose.observability.yaml restart prometheus`. There is no Alertmanager in this
stack: see what fires at http://127.0.0.1:9090/alerts (details in the Forge
observability guide, docs/OBSERVABILITY.md#alert-rules).

The app sends traces, metrics and logs to the collector over OTLP
(`FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` is set by the overlay), which forwards them
to Tempo, Prometheus and Loki. To use another host port set `GRAFANA_PORT` or
`PROMETHEUS_PORT`. The app logs only what it or the framework logs (startup,
errors, your `logger.InfoContext(ctx, ...)` calls); each record made with a traced
context carries `trace_id` and `span_id`, which is what ties logs and traces
together. Output that does not go through the logger (a crash's stack trace, for
example) is only in `docker compose logs`. The
stack keeps seven days of data and is meant for development, not production.
To export from an app started with `forge dev`, run
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME={{.Name}} FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318 forge dev`
while the stack runs, adding `FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp` to send logs too.

## Credentials

Secrets that do not belong in `.env` files live encrypted in
`config/credentials/<environment>.json.enc`, which is committed. The key
stays out of git: `config/credentials/development.key` (created by `forge new`,
mode 0600) or the `FORGE_MASTER_KEY` variable in deployed environments.

```bash
forge credentials edit                          # edit development credentials in $EDITOR
forge credentials show                          # structure only; values are redacted
forge credentials init --environment production # new key and encrypted file for production
FORGE_MASTER_KEY="$(cat config/credentials/production.key)"  # what the deployed app needs
```

Back up every key; without it the encrypted file cannot be read. Read values
with `credentials.Open` from `github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/credentials`.

## Deploy

The image runs `serve` by default and reads `FORGE_*` variables. Production
refuses insecure settings: TLS (or `FORGE_HTTP_TRANSPORT=trusted-proxy` behind
a TLS-terminating proxy){{if and .DB .DB.ProductionTLS}} and {{.DB.ProductionTLS}} on
`FORGE_DATABASE_URL`{{end}}.

Behind a reverse proxy the application sees the proxy's address unless it
trusts that proxy's `X-Forwarded-For`. List the proxy networks so
{{- if .API}}
rate limits and audit records count each client
{{- else}} sign-in
throttles, rate limits and audit records count each client
{{- end}}
(`forge deploy` sets this to the forge-proxy network automatically; empty
trusts nobody):

```bash
docker run --rm -e FORGE_HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.18.0.0/16 {{.Name}}
```
{{- if and .DB (not .DB.Server)}}

The image stores the SQLite database in `/app/storage/production.db`; mount a
persistent volume there and run a single application instance.
{{- end}}
{{- if .DB}}

Run migrations before rolling out a release with the same image:

```bash
{{- if .DevMigrationURL}}
docker run --rm -e FORGE_ENV=production -e FORGE_DATABASE_URL=... -e FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=... {{.Name}} migrate
{{- else}}
docker run --rm -e FORGE_ENV=production -e FORGE_DATABASE_URL=... {{.Name}} migrate
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .DevMigrationURL}}

`FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL` is the role that owns the schema; give it to
this step only. The serving containers get `FORGE_DATABASE_URL` alone, for a
role that is `NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS NOREPLICATION`, owns no table and holds only
`SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE`: in staging and production the app refuses
to start when that role is a superuser or has `BYPASSRLS`, `CREATEROLE` or
`REPLICATION`, is a member of a superuser, `BYPASSRLS` or `REPLICATION` role
or of a predefined role that reaches
the server's files or programs, owns (or can `SET ROLE` to the owner of) a
table with row-level security, or when the URL makes the session act as
another role, because each could bypass the tenant isolation of row-level
security. Provisioning both roles is described
in Forge's
[Database roles](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go/blob/main/docs/DATABASE.md#database-roles-postgresql).
{{- end}}

Concurrent `migrate` runs are serialized by {{.DB.MigrationLock}}.
{{- end}}

## Supply chain: SBOM and signed images

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds the image and keeps a CycloneDX SBOM
of it as the `sbom` artifact of every run. `.github/workflows/release.yml`
publishes a signed image to the GitHub Container Registry for a version tag,
once you opt in; it never runs for pull requests or branch pushes. The image
carries SLSA provenance and an SPDX SBOM, and is signed keyless with Sigstore
cosign through GitHub's OIDC token (no signing key to store).

```bash
# the SBOM of the latest CI run on main
run="$(gh run list --workflow ci.yml --branch main --limit 1 --json databaseId --jq '.[0].databaseId')"
gh run download "$run" --name sbom
jq -r '.components[] | "\(.name) \(.version)"' sbom.cdx.json

gh variable set FORGE_RELEASE_IMAGES --body true  # opt in to publishing
git tag v0.1.0 && git push origin v0.1.0          # build, push and sign

# verify; the image path is OWNER/REPOSITORY in lowercase
cosign verify ghcr.io/OWNER/REPOSITORY:v0.1.0 \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github\.com/OWNER/REPOSITORY/\.github/workflows/release\.yml@refs/tags/v' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/OWNER/REPOSITORY:v0.1.0 --format '{{"{{"}} json .SBOM {{"}}"}}'
docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/OWNER/REPOSITORY:v0.1.0 --format '{{"{{"}} json .Provenance {{"}}"}}'
```
